      "evidence_documents": [
        {
          "byte_length": 206123,
          "canonical_content_sha256": "f6d00874eda0e9a26ace80a88670b42f20da3226ba5b3455b7f1f6466fabe477",
          "canonical_content_text": "Turkiye accuses Israel\u2019s Netanyahu of \u2018genocide\u2019, seeks Interpol warrant | Israel-Palestine conflict News | Al Jazeera Skip links Skip to Content play Live Sign up Show navigation menu Navigation menu News Show more news sections Africa Asia US & Canada Latin America Europe Asia Pacific Middle East Explained Sport Opinion Video More Show more sections Features Economy Human Rights Climate Crisis Investigations Interactives In Pictures Science & Technology Podcasts Travel Sponsored Content play Live Click here to search search Sign up Navigation menu caret-left Israel-Palestine conflict Shackled, bleeding, raped: Abuse in Israeli prisons \u2018This is an apartheid regime\u2019 Does Trump have real leverage over Netanyahu? History of flotilla campaigns caret-right News | Israel-Palestine conflict Turkiye accuses Israel\u2019s Netanyahu of \u2018genocide\u2019, seeks Interpol warrant Netanyahu and Erdogan trade insults as Ankara seeks warrant for Israel\u2019s attacks on Global Sumud Flotilla activists headed for Gaza. Listen Listen (3 mins) Save Click here to share on social media share-nodes Share facebook x whatsapp-stroke copylink google Add Al Jazeera on Google info Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu [File: Ronen Zvulun/Reuters] By Al Jazeera Staff and Reuters Published On 21 Aug 2026 21 Aug 2026 Turkiye has sought an international arrest warrant for Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu, accusing him of \u201cgenocide\u201d for Israel\u2019s attack in May on activists aboard the Global Sumud Flotilla for Gaza . In a post on X on Friday, Turkish Justice Minister Akin Gurlek said arrest warrants had been issued on July 14 for Netanyahu and another suspect. Charges included genocide, crimes against humanity and torture, as part of a case against 35 people over the Israeli interception in international waters. Recommended Stories list of 4 items list 1 of 4 Turkiye unveils its first intercontinental ballistic missile: What we know list 2 of 4 Turkiye, Saudi Arabia, Pakistan sign joint defence agreement: What\u2019s in it? list 3 of 4 NATO summit begins: Who\u2019s attending and what\u2019s at stake? list 4 of 4 Turkiye says Mecca defence pact not aimed at Iran end of list \u201cIn line with the arrest warrants issued against Benjamin Netanyahu \u2026 our justice ministry has requested that \u2060the interior ministry issue [Interpol] red notices for them so that they may be sought \u2060internationally,\u201d Gurlek said. Turkiye will use all available legal mechanisms to ensure Israel is held accountable for its crimes in Gaza, he added. NATO member Turkiye has been one of the fiercest critics of Israel\u2019s genocidal war \u2060on Gaza and has previously issued domestic arrest warrants for Netanyahu over its attacks. Turkiye has also repeatedly called for international measures against Israel for what Ankara calls its violations of human rights and international law. Israel denies those charges. In May, Israeli forces fired on at least two vessels from the Global Sumud Flotilla sailing towards \u200cGaza. It was making a renewed attempt to deliver aid to the enclave after earlier missions were intercepted by the Israeli military in international waters. Israel\u2019s interception of the flotilla provoked international outcry. France, Italy and Australia have also launched judicial proceedings. Advertisement About 50 vessels were in the Global Sumud Flotilla that left Turkiye in May heading for the besieged Palestinian territory. The activists were detained in the Mediterranean by the Israeli military and then taken to an Israeli prison before being deported . Israel\u2019s far right National Security Minister, Itamar Ben-Gvir caused outrage at the time by releasing a video of himself angrily provoking flotilla activists who were on their knees with their hands tied behind their backs. \u201cWe will not allow the crimes committed in Gaza to be covered up or those responsible to be protected by a shield of impunity,\u201d Gurlek said. \u201cWe will resolutely use all means available under national and international law.\u201d Asked about Gurlek\u2019s statement, Netanyahu\u2019s office said: \u201c[Turkish President] Erdogan\u2019s pathetic attempt to intimidate \u2060the leaders and soldiers of Israel, the only true democracy in the \u2060Middle East, will go nowhere.\u201d His office accused Erdogan of being \u201cantisemitic\u201d and vowed that Israel would \u201ccontinue to act forcefully against Turkiye\u2019s attempts to destabilise the region\u201d. Soon after, in a post on X, Erdogan\u2019s office denounced Netanyahu\u2019s \u201cpetty political tactics\u201d and condemned what it called the Israeli government\u2019s attempt \u201cto present Turkiye as a new threat\u201d. aj-logo aj-logo aj-logo Advertisement About About Show more About Us Code of Ethics Terms and Conditions EU/EEA Regulatory Notice Privacy Policy Cookie Policy Cookie Preferences Accessibility Statement Sitemap Work for us Connect Connect Show more Contact Us User Accounts Help Advertise with us Stay Connected Newsletters Channel Finder TV Schedule Podcasts Submit a Tip Paid Partner Content Our Channels Our Channels Show more Al Jazeera Arabic Al Jazeera English Al Jazeera Investigative Unit Al Jazeera Mubasher Al Jazeera Documentary Al Jazeera Balkans AJ+ Our Network Our Network Show more Al Jazeera Centre for Studies Al Jazeera Media Institute Learn Arabic Al Jazeera Centre for Public Liberties & Human Rights Al Jazeera Forum Al Jazeera Hotel Partners Follow Al Jazeera English: facebook twitter youtube instagram-colored-outline rss \u00a9 2026 Al Jazeera Media Network",
